= Solution
{parent=i}

**<Carbon dioxide> is linear, with two equivalent C–O bonds of formal <bond order> two; its <HOMO> is predominantly oxygen nonbonding and its <LUMO> is C–O $\pi^*$ antibonding.** The carbon uses two opposite $sp$ hybrids for its <sigma bonds> and two perpendicular p <atomic orbitals> for <pi bonding>. A localized $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ picture assigns each terminal oxygen an $sp^2$ sigma/lone-pair framework and one p orbital for its local pi bond.

In a symmetry-adapted description, each of the two perpendicular sets of oxygen and carbon p functions forms bonding, oxygen-centred nonbonding and antibonding combinations. The occupied nonbonding pair is labelled $1\pi_g$ and the lowest empty pi pair $2\pi_u^*$. The latter has appreciable amplitude at carbon, explaining its electrophilic reactivity. Hybridization of a terminal atom with only one bonded neighbour is a choice of localized basis, not an independently measurable bond angle; an alternative oxygen $sp$ plus p basis can represent the same delocalized system.

There are 16 <valence electrons>: four in the two sigma bonds, four in the two net pi bonds, and eight in the remaining oxygen lone-pair combinations. These local electron counts and the delocalized orbital description are alternative bookkeeping schemes, not additional electrons.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**<Ozone> is bent, with two equivalent O–O bonds of approximate <bond order> $1.5$.** Its useful localized <orbital hybridization> description is $sp^2$ at all three oxygens. Two hybrids at the central oxygen form sigma bonds and a third contains a <lone pair>; the three perpendicular p orbitals form a <three-centre four-electron pi bond>.

The three pi orbitals are bonding, nonbonding and antibonding, filled as $\pi_{\rm b}^2\pi_{\rm nb}^2\pi_*^0$. The terminal-centred nonbonding orbital is the <HOMO>, conventionally $1a_2$; the delocalized antibonding orbital is the <LUMO>, $2b_1$. The two principal <resonance structures>, $\mathrm{^-O-O^+=O}$ and $\mathrm{O=O^+-O^-}$, distribute the extra pi bond across the two links.

The 18 valence electrons comprise four sigma-bond electrons, ten in five sigma-framework lone pairs, and four in the pi system. This gives a paired ground-state model. More accurate treatments include electron correlation; the simple bond-order picture is not an exact charge-density calculation.<The Structures, Molecular Orbital Properties and Vibrational Spectra of the Homo- and Heterodimers of Sulphur Dioxide and Ozone. An Ab Initio Study by Thomas A. Ford>[2021, §2.3 and Table 9: ozone HOMO and LUMO]{ref}

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**The central carbon in <carbon dioxide> has two bonding directions and no lone pair, whereas the central oxygen in <ozone> has two bonding directions and one lone pair.** In the <VSEPR model>, carbon dioxide therefore has a linear arrangement; ozone has an approximately trigonal-planar electron-domain arrangement but a bent atomic skeleton. Multiple bonding occupies one direction for this geometrical count. The extra central-oxygen lone pair, rather than a different number of atomic nuclei, creates the difference.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**The product is <carbonic acid>, $\mathrm{HO-C(=O)-OH}$.** The oxygen <lone pair> <HOMO> of water donates into the carbon-centred component of the carbon dioxide $\pi^*$ <LUMO>. Draw an <electron-pair arrow> from the water oxygen to carbon and a second from one C=O pi bond onto its oxygen:
$$
\mathrm{CO_2+H_2O\longrightarrow\ ^-O-C(=O)-OH_2^+\longrightarrow HO-C(=O)-OH}.
$$
The first step bends the original linear electrophile and gives carbon three oxygen neighbours; carbon is approximately trigonal planar in the resulting carbonate framework. Proton transfer from the oxonium oxygen to the negatively charged oxygen produces the neutral acid. In real water, solvent molecules relay that proton rather than requiring a strained isolated intramolecular transfer. The forward mechanism does not assert that most dissolved carbon dioxide is present as carbonic acid at equilibrium.

\Image[paper-1-hydration-protonation.png]
{title=Original <electron-pair arrows> for carbon dioxide hydration and protonation routes from the tricarbide ion}

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**The elementary valence-MO model gives $\boxed{\text{bond order}(\mathrm C_2)=2}$.** Each carbon supplies four $n=2$ valence electrons, so fill eight electrons in the ordering appropriate to the lighter second-row diatomics:
$$
(\sigma_g2s)^2(\sigma_u^*2s)^2(\pi_u2p)^4(\sigma_g2p)^0(\pi_g^*2p)^0(\sigma_u^*2p)^0.
$$
The two $\pi_u$ orbitals are degenerate, as are the two $\pi_g^*$ orbitals. The $2s$ bonding and antibonding occupations cancel; four net p-bonding electrons remain, giving $(6-2)/2=2$. Mixing between sigma orbitals of compatible symmetry places the occupied $\pi_u$ pair below the empty p-derived $\sigma_g$ orbital in this diagram. All occupied orbitals are paired.

\Image[paper-1-carbon-orbitals.png]
{title=Original valence <molecular orbital> energy diagram for carbon dimer, with atomic inputs and symmetry labels}

The omitted $1s$ orbitals form filled $\sigma_g1s$ and $\sigma_u^*1s$ levels. Their contributions to the formal bond order cancel, and their low energies and small spatial extent make them largely core-like. Omitting them simplifies this valence diagram without claiming they do not exist or have exactly no effect on molecular energies.

= e
{parent=2}
