**The most acidic sites are: acetic-acid O–H, $\mathrm pK_a\approx4.8$; phenol O–H, $\approx10$; ethanal alpha-CH$_3$, $\approx17$–$20$; propyne terminal ≡C–H, $\approx25$.** These are ordinary approximate organic-chemistry estimates; exact acidities depend on solvent and reference convention.

<Acetic acid> gives a carboxylate in which negative charge is delocalized over two oxygens through equivalent <resonance structures>. <Phenol> gives a phenoxide with delocalization into the aromatic system, but its important contributors are less equivalent and do not stabilize the charge as effectively as a carboxylate.

For ethanal, removal of a methyl hydrogen gives the <enolate> $\mathrm{^-CH_2-CH=O\leftrightarrow CH_2=CH-O^-}$. The aldehydic hydrogen is not the preferred acidic site: removing it does not give this alpha-carbon/oxygen delocalization. For <propyne>, loss of the terminal hydrogen gives an acetylide with a lone pair in an $sp$ orbital. Its greater s character stabilizes negative charge relative to an ordinary $sp^2$ or $sp^3$ carbanion; the methyl hydrogens are much less acidic.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**The first transformation is two successive dehydrohalogenations; the second opens the lactone by two methyl additions and then closes a six-membered cyclic ether.** Both schemes must be accounted for within this part.

For the dichloride, write $R=\mathrm{cyclohexyl}$. In the product-forming path, <tert-butoxide> removes a hydrogen from the methyl group of $\mathrm{R-CCl_2-CH_3}$. Draw arrows from the base lone pair to H, from C–H into the C–C bond, and from C–Cl to Cl. This <E2 elimination> gives $\mathrm{R-C(Cl)=CH_2}$. A second base-induced elimination removes a vinyl hydrogen and the remaining chloride, forming $\mathrm{R-C\equiv CH}$. The second elimination is more demanding because a vinylic bond is involved, consistent with the strong-base, heated conditions. This route explains the displayed product without asserting that no other elimination regioisomer can ever form.

For the lactone, a methyl equivalent from <methylmagnesium iodide> attacks the ester carbonyl carbon, with its pi pair moving to oxygen. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate reforms C=O and cleaves the acyl–oxygen bond, opening the ring to the magnesium alkoxide corresponding to $\mathrm{HO(CH_2)_4C(=O)CH_3}$. The newly formed <ketone> is more susceptible to a second methyl addition; its pi pair again moves to oxygen. After water workup, the product is the diol $\mathrm{HO(CH_2)_4C(OH)(CH_3)_2}$.

Under acid and heat, protonate its tertiary OH, then move the C–O pair onto oxygen as water leaves. The resulting $\mathrm{HO(CH_2)_4C^+(CH_3)_2}$ is trapped intramolecularly by the remaining primary-alcohol oxygen. Its lone pair forms the closing O–C bond, producing a six-membered cyclic oxonium ion. Deprotonation yields **2,2-dimethyloxane**, also called 2,2-dimethyltetrahydropyran. Ring closure retains four chain methylenes; it does not form a five-membered ring. The two methyl groups both derive from the two equivalents of Grignard reagent.

\Image[paper-1-organic-mechanisms.png]
{title=Original electron-flow schemes for double elimination, lactone addition and acid-induced ether formation}

