**The alcohol is oxidized to <cyclohexanone> through an alkyl hypochlorite intermediate.** Acid converts hypochlorite into electrophilic hypochlorous acid. The alcohol oxygen attacks chlorine, with acid-assisted proton transfers and loss of water giving the O–Cl derivative $\mathrm{R_2CH-O-Cl}$.

A base such as acetate then removes the hydrogen attached to the carbinol carbon. The three coupled electron-pair motions are: base → H; C–H → C–O to form C=O; and O–Cl → Cl to release chloride. The resulting carbonyl is cyclohexanone. This is an elimination-like oxidation of the hypochlorite ester: the original alcohol oxygen becomes the ketone oxygen, while loss of its O-bound H and the carbinol C-bound H effects the net oxidation.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Methyl addition gives 1-methylcyclohexanol, whose acid-catalysed dehydration yields the shown 1-methylcyclohexene.** The methyl end of <methyllithium> attacks the cyclohexanone carbonyl carbon, and the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen, giving a lithium alkoxide. Protonation gives the tertiary alcohol.

Protonate OH to make $\mathrm{OH_2^+}$, then cleave its C–O bond with both electrons going to departing water. A base removes a beta hydrogen from either adjacent ring methylene, and that C–H pair forms the C=C bond to the tertiary cationic centre. This <E1 elimination> regenerates the acid catalyst. The two adjacent ring positions are equivalent, and the displayed endocyclic alkene is more substituted than the possible exocyclic methylene alternative.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**D is racemic trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol. E is racemic 1-methyl-2-phenylcyclohexan-1-ol with OH and Ph trans, and Me and Ph cis.** Number the starting alkene carbon carrying methyl as $\mathrm C_1$ and its partner as $\mathrm C_2$ in the reaction explanation below; the product name of D renumbers the OH-bearing carbon as 1.

For D, <hydroboration> proceeds through a concerted four-centre transition state: the alkene pi pair donates to boron while B–H supplies the new C–H bond. Boron attaches mainly to less substituted $\mathrm C_2$ and hydrogen to $\mathrm C_1$, on the same face. Under peroxide/base conditions, hydroperoxide attacks B; migration of the C–B bond to the adjacent oxygen, with O–O cleavage, replaces C–B by C–O with retention at carbon. Hydrolysis/proton transfer releases the alcohol. Thus the newly added H and OH are syn, making the pre-existing methyl and OH trans. Attack on either alkene face gives the enantiomeric pair.

For E, the peracid transfers its terminal oxygen across the alkene in concerted <epoxidation>, through cyclic electron flow that also breaks O–O and transfers the acidic proton to the peracid carbonyl oxygen. The resulting <epoxide> retains the relative configuration of the alkene substituents. The phenyl equivalent from <phenylmagnesium bromide> then attacks less substituted $\mathrm C_2$ from the face opposite the epoxide oxygen. Its C–O bond breaks onto oxygen, an SN2-like ring opening; the oxygen stays attached to $\mathrm C_1$. Water workup protonates that alkoxide. Consequently OH at $\mathrm C_1$ and Ph at $\mathrm C_2$ are anti, while the methyl at $\mathrm C_1$ lies on the same relative face as Ph.

\Image[paper-1-stereochemical-products.png]
{title=Original product structures showing the relative stereochemistry of hydroboration–oxidation and epoxide opening}

