The decadic <Beer-Lambert law> can be written
$$
\boxed{I=I_0\,10^{-\varepsilon lc},\qquad \log_{10}(I_0/I)=\varepsilon lc.}
$$
Here $l$ is the path length in metres, $c$ is concentration in $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ or M, and $\varepsilon$ is the decadic <molar attenuation coefficient> in $\mathrm{M^{-1}\,m^{-1}}$. The intensity ratio and the exponent are dimensionless. Equivalently $I=I_0e^{-\kappa lc}$ with $\kappa=(\ln10)\varepsilon$ in the same units. Defining the logarithm convention avoids a missing factor of $\ln10$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The <absorbance> is $\boxed{A=\log_{10}(I_0/I)=-\log_{10}(I/I_0)}$.** It is dimensionless. It differs from the absorbed fraction $1-I/I_0$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a <first-order reaction> with no appreciable reverse contribution in this decay experiment,
$$
\frac{dc}{dt}=-kc,\qquad c(t)=c_0e^{-kt}.
$$
With fixed path length and attenuation coefficient, and absorbance attributable to ONOO after blank/background correction, the <Beer-Lambert law> gives $A(t)=\varepsilon lc_0e^{-kt}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\ln A(t)=\ln(\varepsilon lc_0)-kt.}
$$
**The gradient is $-k$**, in $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ when time is measured in seconds. Constant illumination wavelength, homogeneous sampling and negligible product absorbance at that wavelength are required for this direct interpretation.

