= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The intermediate balance is
$$
\frac{d[\mathrm{ONOO}]}{dt}=k_1[\mathrm{NO}][\mathrm{O_2}]-k_{-1}[\mathrm{ONOO}]-k_2[\mathrm{NO}][\mathrm{ONOO}].
$$
Setting this derivative approximately to zero under the <quasi-steady-state approximation> gives
$$
\boxed{[\mathrm{ONOO}]_{\rm ss}=\frac{k_1[\mathrm{NO}][\mathrm{O_2}]}{k_{-1}+k_2[\mathrm{NO}]}.}
$$
Both dissociation and reaction with NO remove the intermediate. Its steady concentration is not zero and does not require the first reversible pair to be at equilibrium.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The last <elementary reaction> creates two nitrogen dioxide molecules per event, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{d[\mathrm{NO_2}]}{dt}=2k_2[\mathrm{NO}][\mathrm{ONOO}]_{\rm ss}
=\frac{2k_1k_2[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{O_2}]}{k_{-1}+k_2[\mathrm{NO}]}.}
$$
The factor of two is required for the production rate requested. The conventional reaction rate for $\mathrm{2NO+O_2\to2NO_2}$ would be one half of this production rate.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

“Low NO” means $k_2[\mathrm{NO}]\ll k_{-1}$, or $[\mathrm{NO}]\ll6.5\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm M$. Then
$$
\frac{d[\mathrm{NO_2}]}{dt}\simeq\frac{2k_1k_2}{k_{-1}}[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{O_2}].
$$
**The effective orders are two in NO and one in oxygen, giving overall order three.** For the production-rate convention,
$$
\boxed{k_{\rm eff,prod}=\frac{2(189)(1.0\times10^9)}{6.5\times10^3}=5.82\times10^7\,\mathrm{M^{-2}\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
If the conventional reaction rate $v=\tfrac12d[\mathrm{NO_2}]/dt$ is used, its coefficient is $2.91\times10^7\,\mathrm{M^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$. These are the same kinetics under explicitly different normalizations. At high NO the denominator changes, so the effective NO order approaches one rather than remaining two.

= c
